// Lesson 3.5 — Player Projectile: Clone, Retag & Reuse the Cooldown
// Taught 2026-10-08. The row has existed in curriculum.ts since the calendar was
// rewritten; the page did not. The player stops being only a target.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read from the teacher's project):
//   - Assets/Prefabs/Player_Projectile.prefab is a clone of Turret_Projectile:
//     tag `Fireball` (the enemy one is `E_Fireball`), CircleCollider2D with
//     m_IsTrigger: 1 and m_Radius: 0.07, no Rigidbody2D. Same sprite, recolored.
//   - Assets/Scripts/PlayerProjectile.cs: `float speed = 10;`, a `Vector3
//     direction;`, `float lifespan = 2;`, an Update that counts the lifespan down
//     to Destroy and then does `transform.position += direction * speed *
//     Time.deltaTime;` — transform movement, not a body, exactly like the turret
//     bullet it was cloned from. `public void SetDirection(String facing)` maps
//     "Right"/"Left"/"Up"/"Down" to a unit Vector3. There is no default case, so a
//     null or unexpected facing leaves direction at (0,0,0) and the bullet sits
//     still until its lifespan eats it.
//   - PlayerController: `[SerializeField] GameObject projectile;` wired in
//     Player.prefab to the Player_Projectile prefab, `String facing;` written by
//     the direction block in Update(), and
//         void Shoot() {
//             GameObject playerProjectileCopy = Instantiate(projectile,
//                 transform.position, Quaternion.identity);
//             playerProjectileCopy.GetComponent<PlayerProjectile>().SetDirection(facing);
//         }
//     fired from `if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space) && cooldownTimer < 0)`.
//   - NOTHING in the project receives the `Fireball` tag yet. This page must not
//     claim the player's bullet damages anything. 4.2/4.5 make the enemy care.
//
// LADDER: level 2 — every shape here has a precedent (3.2 built Instantiate, 3.3
// built the cooldown, 1.6 built `facing`). One new idea is left blank: the line
// that hands the spawned copy its direction.
