        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Player_Projectile → Tag</strong>: set it to <code>Fireball</code>.
            The turret's bullet is <code>E_Fireball</code>, and that tag is the only thing that will
            ever tell the two apart. If <code>Fireball</code> is not in the list, add it in
            <strong>Tags and Layers</strong> first.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Sprite Renderer → Color</strong>: tint it. A bullet the player cannot
            tell from the enemy's is a bullet the player will run into.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Scripts</code> → right-click →
            <strong>Create → C# Script</strong> → <code>PlayerProjectile</code>. Note the name matches
            the class inside it — Unity requires that, and a mismatch stops the script from attaching.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Player_Projectile</strong> → <strong>Add Component → Player Projectile</strong>,
            then remove the old <code>Projectile</code> component. One bullet, one script. Two scripts
            both moving the same object is a bug that looks like a framerate problem.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select <strong>Player</strong> → <strong>Inspector → Player
            Controller</strong>. Because the Player in a scene is a prefab instance, an empty
            <code>Projectile</code> slot here is an <em>override</em> — click <strong>Overrides →
