        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it gives you</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Instantiate(prefab, position, Quaternion.identity)</code></td>
            <td>A live copy of the prefab, and the copy itself back as a return value so you can configure it.</td>
            <td>Every copy is a new object in the scene — nothing cleans them up for you.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>GetComponent&lt;PlayerProjectile&gt;()</code></td>
            <td>The component on the object at the other end of the reference.</td>
            <td>It returns <code>null</code> if the object has no such component — and then throws on the next dot.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Vector3 direction * speed * Time.deltaTime</code></td>
            <td>A movement step that is frame-independent and points anywhere you like.</td>
            <td>A direction that is not unit length quietly changes the speed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>CompareTag("Fireball")</code></td>
            <td>The cheapest way for something else to know what hit it.</td>
            <td>Nothing on the other side has it yet — the tag only starts working when a receiver asks.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>The tag is doing nothing yet, and that is on purpose.</strong> Nothing in this project
        reacts to <code>Fireball</code> today, so your bullet passes through every enemy in the game.
        It is not broken — it is waiting for a receiver. The enemy that flinches when it is hit arrives
        in Unit 4, and the tag you set today is the handle it will grab.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Whose Job Is Aiming?</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The turret's bullet seeks a target it was handed; the player's bullet moves in a direction it was told. Both were written by you. Which model is better for a weapon the player aims — and what does the turret's version get that yours cannot?</li>
            <li><code>SetDirection</code> takes a <code>String</code>. What would you have to change if the player could face eight directions instead of four, and where would that change have to happen?</li>
            <li>Your bullet and the turret's are two nearly identical files. What is the argument for keeping them separate, and what is the argument for one script with a flag?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

